#import "main-defs.typ": *
#import "statements.typ": *

== The Jacobson radical <sec:jacobson-radical>

#definition[
  The *socle*#idx("socle", "of a module")#idx("module", "socle of") of a
  $Lambda$-module $M$ is the sum of all the irreducible submodules of $M$, and
  is written $Soc(M)$. The *socle layers*#idx("socle", "series, layer, length")
  of $M$ are defined inductively by $Soc^0(M) = 0$,
  $Soc^n (M) slash Soc^(n-1)(M) = Soc(M slash Soc^(n-1)(M))$.

  The *radical*#idx("radical", "of a module")#idx("module", "radical of") of $M$
  is the intersection of all the maximal submodules of $M$, and is written
  $Rad(M)$. The *radical series*#idx("radical", "series, length") or *Loewy
  series*#idx("Loewy", "series")#idx("series", "Loewy") of $M$ is defined
  inductively by $Rad^0(M) = M$, $Rad^n (M) = Rad(Rad^(n-1)(M))$. The $n$th
  *radical layer*#idx("radical", "layers")#idx("layer", "radical") or *Loewy
  layer*#idx(
    "Loewy",
    "layer",
  )#idx("layer", "Loewy") is $Rad^(n-1)(M) slash Rad^n (M)$.

  The module $M$ is said to be *completely reducible*#idx(
    "completely reducible",
  )#idx("reducible, completely") or *semisimple*#idx(
    "semisimple",
    "module",
  )#idx("module", "semisimple") if $M = Soc(M)$. This is equivalent to the
  condition that every submodule has a complement, by Zorn's lemma. If $M$
  satisfies D.C.C. then $M$ is completely reducible if and only if $Rad(M) = 0$.
  In this case, $M$ is a finite direct sum of irreducible modules.

  The *head*#idx("head of a module")#idx("module", "head of") or *top*#idx(
    "top of a module",
  )#idx("module", "top of") of $M$ is $name("Head")(M) = M slash Rad(M)$.

  If $M$ has a composition series and *socle length*#idx("length", "socle") $n$
  (i.e., $Soc^n (M) = M$ but $Soc^(n-1)(M) != M$) then $M$ also has *radical
  length*#idx(
    "length",
    "radical",
  )#idx("radical", "length") $n$ (i.e., $Rad^n (M) = 0$ but $Rad^(n-1)(M) != 0$)
  and $Soc^j (M) supset.eq Rad^(n-j)(M)$ for all $0 lt.eq j lt.eq n$.
] <def:socle-radical>

The *annihilator*#idx("annihilator") of an element $m in M$ is the set of all
elements $lambda in Lambda$ with $lambda m = 0$. It is a left ideal, which is
maximal#idx("ideal", "maximal")#idx("maximal ideal") if and only if the
submodule generated by $m$ is irreducible. The annihilator of $M$ is defined to
be the intersection of the annihilators of the elements of $M$. It is a two
sided ideal $I$. If $M$ is irreducible then $I$ is *primitive*#idx(
  "primitive",
  "ideal",
)#idx("ideal", "primitive"), meaning that $Lambda slash I$ has a faithful
irreducible module. We define $J(Lambda)$, the *Jacobson radical*#idx(
  "Jacobson radical",
)#idx(
  "radical",
  "Jacobson",
) of $Lambda$ to be the intersection of the maximal left ideals, or equivalently
the intersection of the primitive two sided ideals.

We claim that $J(Lambda)$ consists of those elements $x in Lambda$ such that
$1 - a x b$ has a two sided inverse for all $a, b in Lambda$, so that it does
not matter whether we use left or right ideals to define $J(Lambda)$. If
$x in J(Lambda)$ then $1 - x$ cannot be in any maximal left ideal (since
otherwise 1 would be!) so it has a left inverse, say $t(1 - x) = 1$. Then
$1 - t = -t x in J(Lambda)$ so $t$ has a left inverse, and is hence a two sided
inverse for $1 - x$. Applying this with $a x b$ in place of $x$ shows that
$1 - a x b$ has a two sided inverse. Conversely, if $1 - a x b$ has a two sided
inverse for all $a, b in Lambda$, and $I$ is a maximal left ideal not containing
$x$, then we can write 1 as $a x$ plus an element of $I$, contradicting the
invertibility of $1 - a x$.

If we let $Lambda$ act on itself as a left module, we call this the *regular
representation*#idx("representation", "regular")#idx(
  "regular",
  "representation",
) $lmodule(Lambda, Lambda)$#idx("$lmodule(Lambda, Lambda)$"). Since submodules
are the same as left ideals, we have $J(Lambda) = Rad(lmodule(Lambda, Lambda))$.
We say that $Lambda$ is *semisimple*#idx("semisimple", "ring")#idx(
  "ring",
  "semisimple",
) if $J(Lambda) = 0$. Note that $Lambda slash J(Lambda)$ is always semisimple.

#lemma[
  If $a in J(Lambda)$ then $1 - a$ has a left inverse in $Lambda$.
] <lem:radical-unit>

#proof[
  #source(16)Since $1 = a + (1 - a)$ we have
  $Lambda = J(Lambda) + Lambda(1 - a)$. If $Lambda(1 - a) != Lambda$, then by
  Zorn's lemma there is a maximal left ideal $I$ with
  $Lambda(1 - a) subset.eq I$. By definition of $J(Lambda)$ we also have
  $J(Lambda) subset.eq I$, and so $Lambda subset.eq I$. This contradiction
  proves the lemma.
]

#lemma(title: [Nakayama])[
  If $M$ is a finitely generated $Lambda$-module and $J(Lambda) M = M$ then
  $M = 0$.
] <lem:nakayama>
#idx("Nakayama", "lemma", target: [@lem:nakayama])#idx(
  "lemma",
  "Nakayama's",
  target: [@lem:nakayama],
)

#proof[
  Suppose that $M != 0$. Choose $m_1, dots, m_n$ generating $M$ with $n$
  minimal. Since $J(Lambda) M = M$, we can write $m_n = sum_(i=1)^n a_i m_i$
  with $a_i in J(Lambda)$. By Lemma~@lem:radical-unit, $1 - a_n$ has a left
  inverse $b$ in $Lambda$. Then $(1 - a_n) m_n = sum_(i=1)^(n-1) a_i m_i$, and
  so $m_n = b(sum_(i=1)^(n-1) a_i m_i)$, contradicting the minimality of $n$.
]

#lemma[
  If $Lambda$ is semisimple and satisfies D.C.C. on left ideals, then every
  $Lambda$-module is completely reducible. Conversely if $Lambda$ satisfies
  D.C.C. on left ideals and $lmodule(Lambda, Lambda)$ is completely reducible
  then $Lambda$ is semisimple.
] <lem:semisimple-ring>

#proof[
  If $Lambda$ is semisimple then $Rad(lmodule(Lambda, Lambda)) = J(Lambda) = 0$
  and so $lmodule(Lambda, Lambda)$ is completely reducible. Choosing a set of
  generators for a module displays it as a quotient of a direct sum of copies of
  $lmodule(Lambda, Lambda)$, and hence every module is completely reducible.
  Conversely if $lmodule(Lambda, Lambda)$ is completely reducible then
  $lmodule(Lambda, Lambda) tilde.equiv Lambda slash J(Lambda) plus.o
  J(Lambda)$ and so as $Lambda$-modules,
  $
    J(Lambda) = J(Lambda) . (Lambda slash J(Lambda) plus.o J(Lambda))
    = J(Lambda) . J(Lambda)
  $
  so that by Nakayama's lemma $J(Lambda) = 0$.
]

#proposition[
  If $Lambda slash J(Lambda)$ satisfies D.C.C. on left ideals and $M$ is a
  finitely generated $Lambda$-module then $J(Lambda) M = Rad(M)$.
] <prop:radical-of-module>

#proof[
  If $M'$ is a maximal submodule of $M$ then by Nakayama's lemma we have
  $J(Lambda)(M slash M') = 0$ so that $J(Lambda) M subset.eq M'$, and hence
  $J(Lambda) M subset.eq Rad(M)$. Conversely $M slash J(Lambda) M$ is completely
  reducible by Lemma~@lem:semisimple-ring and so $Rad(M slash J(Lambda) M) = 0$,
  which implies that $Rad(M) subset.eq J(Lambda) M$.
]

#definition[
  A ring $Lambda$ is said to be *Noetherian*#idx("Noetherian", "ring")#idx(
    "ring",
    "Noetherian",
  ) if it satisfies A.C.C. on left ideals, and *Artinian*#idx(
    "ring",
    "Artinian",
  ) if it satisfies D.C.C. on left ideals. A $Lambda$-module is
  Noetherian/Artinian if it satisfies A.C.C./D.C.C. on submodules.
] <def:noetherian-artinian>

#theorem[
  If $Lambda$ is Artinian then
  + $J(Lambda)$ is nilpotent.#idx("nilpotence of $J(Lambda)$")
  + If $M$ is a finitely generated $Lambda$-module then $M$ is both Noetherian
    and Artinian.
  + $Lambda$ is Noetherian.#idx("Artinian ring (is Noetherian)")
] <th:artinian-ring>

#proof[
  + Since $Lambda$ satisfies D.C.C. on left ideals, for some $n$ we have
    $J(Lambda)^n = J(Lambda)^(2n)$. If $J(Lambda)^n != 0$, then again using
    D.C.C. we see that there is a minimal left ideal $I$ with
    $J(Lambda)^n I != 0$. Choose $x in I$ with $J(Lambda)^n . x != 0$, and in
    particular $x != 0$. Then $I = J(Lambda)^n . x$ by minimality of $I$, and so
    for #source(17)some $a in J(Lambda)^n$, we have $x = a x$. But then
    $(1 - a) x = 0$, and so $x = 0$ by Lemma~@lem:radical-unit.
  + Let $M_i = J(Lambda)^i M$. Then $M_i slash M_(i+1)$ is annihilated by
    $J(Lambda)$, and is hence completely reducible by
    Lemma~@lem:semisimple-ring. Since $M$ is a finitely generated module over an
    Artinian ring, it satisfies D.C.C., and hence so does $M_i slash M_(i+1)$.
    Thus $M_i slash M_(i+1)$ is a finite direct sum of irreducible modules, and
    so it satisfies A.C.C. It follows that $M$ also satisfies A.C.C.
  + This follows by applying (ii) to the module $lmodule(Lambda, Lambda)$.
]

The following proposition shows that whether a ring homomorphism to an Artinian
ring is surjective can be detected modulo the square of the radical.

#proposition[
  Suppose that $Lambda$ is an Artinian ring and $Lambda'$ is a subring of
  $Lambda$ such that $Lambda' + J^2(Lambda) = Lambda$. Then $Lambda' = Lambda$.
] <prop:surjective-mod-radical-square>

#proof[
  We show that $Lambda' + J^n (Lambda) = Lambda' + J^(n+1)(Lambda)$ for
  $n gt.eq 2$, so that by induction and part~(i) of the previous theorem we
  deduce that $Lambda' = Lambda$. If $x in J^(n-1)(Lambda)$ and
  $y in J(Lambda)$, choose $x' in J^(n-1)(Lambda) inter Lambda'$ such that
  $x - x' in J^n (Lambda)$ and $y' in J(Lambda) inter Lambda'$ such that
  $y - y' in J^2(Lambda)$. Then
  $
    x y & = x(y - y') + (x - x') y' + x' y' \
        & in J^(n-1)(Lambda) J^2(Lambda) + J^n (Lambda)(J(Lambda) inter Lambda')
          + (J^(n-1)(Lambda) inter Lambda')(J(Lambda) inter Lambda') \
        & subset.eq J^(n+1)(Lambda) + Lambda'.
  $
]

#exercises[
  + If $Lambda$ is a Noetherian ring, show that a $Lambda$-module is finitely
    generated if and only if it is Noetherian. Deduce that every submodule of a
    finitely generated $Lambda$-module is finitely generated.
  + Give an example of a simple ring (i.e., one with no non-trivial two sided
    ideals) which is not Noetherian.
]
